.TH SU 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
su \- run a shell with elevated privileges
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B su
.RB [ \-tW ]
.RB [ \-N " | " \-s
.IR shell ]
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.B root
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B su
.RB [ \-tW ]
.RB [ \-N " | " \-s
.IR shell ]
.B \-c
.I command
.RB [[ \-\- ]
.B root
.RI [ arg0
.RI [ argument ...]]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B su
starts a shell with administrator rights, in a new console window.
Unless
.B su
is already elevated, Windows shows its User Account Control (UAC)
prompt, and when the prompt is accepted the shell runs elevated.
Elevation is all that
.B su
does: it does not switch users, ask for a password, or read a user
database; see
.BR NOTES .
This
.B su
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
By default the shell is the kit's
.BR sh (1),
run from the
.I busybox.exe
that is running
.BR su .
Its window is titled
.BR "BusyBox ash (Admin)" ,
and it starts in the current directory of
.BR su .
.B \-s
chooses another program.
.PP
The first operand, if there is one, names the user and must be
.BR root .
The other operands are passed to the shell as its arguments.
With no
.B \-c
and no arguments, the shell is interactive.
With arguments but no
.BR \-c ,
the first is a script for the default shell to run.
With
.BR \-c ,
the shell runs
.I command
and exits, and the first argument is the name of the command, which is
.B $0
in
.BR sh (1).
.PP
.B su
reads no standard input, and writes to standard output only for
.BR \-\-help .
The elevated shell is a separate process in a console window of its own,
so
.B su
can neither feed it input nor collect its output.
Unless
.B \-W
or
.B \-t
is given,
.B su
exits as soon as Windows has started the shell.
The window closes when the shell exits, so the output of a command given
with
.B \-c
is lost unless
.B \-N
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-tW ,
and may follow operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so that arguments for the shell can begin with a dash.
An unknown option, an option without its argument, or both
.B \-N
and
.B \-s
is a usage error:
.B su
prints a usage message on standard error and exits with status 1.
.TP
.BI "\-c " command
Run
.I command
in the shell, which then exits with the status of the command.
The shell is given
.B \-c
.IR command ,
or
.B /c
.I command
when the name of its file is
.B cmd.exe
in any case.
Operands after the user name follow
.I command
as arguments.
.TP
.BI "\-s " shell
Run
.I shell
instead of
.BR "busybox ash" .
.I shell
is the path of a program, with forward slashes or backslashes.
It is not searched for in
.BR PATH :
a name without a directory is looked for in the current directory.
If that is not an executable file, the suffixes
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
and
.B .cmd
are tried in that order, unless the name already ends with one of them
or with a period.
When no executable file is found,
.B su
exits with status 1.
.IP
The shell gets only the command, if any, and the operands.
It gets no window title, and
.B su
passes no starting directory.
Incompatible with
.BR \-N .
.TP
.B \-N
Do not close the console window when the shell exits.
Before it exits, the shell prints
.B Press any key to exit...\&
and waits for a key, unless it ends by running a command with its
.B exec
builtin.
Incompatible with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.B \-t
Test mode: do not elevate.
The shell is an ordinary process that shares the console, standard
handles, and environment of
.BR su ,
and is given no window title.
Any user name is accepted and ignored, and the test for dropped
privilege described in
.B NOTES
is skipped.
Implies
.BR \-W .
.TP
.B \-W
Wait for the shell to exit, and exit with its status; see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and exit
with status 0.
Any further arguments are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B su
reads no environment variables of its own, but code that it shares
reads these:
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
The C library's option parser reads it.
When it is set, even to an empty value, options are recognized only
before the first operand, and later ones are passed to the shell.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
The variables that the shell reads are described in
.BR sh (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Without
.B \-W
or
.BR \-t ,
the shell was started.
With them, the shell exited with status 0.
.TP
.B 1
A usage error, a user name other than
.BR root ,
a
.B \-s
program that was not found, or a failure to start the shell.
With
.B \-W
or
.BR \-t ,
also a status of 1 from the shell, and a failure to read its status.
.TP
.B 2
The privilege of
.B su
was dropped and cannot be restored.
The shell is not started.
.PP
Errors are reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
su: unknown user \(aq\fIname\fR\(aq
su: \fIshell\fR: Not found
su: unable to restore privilege
.EE
.RE
.PP
A failure to start the shell has no message from
.BR su ,
as when the UAC prompt is declined, although Windows may show its own.
.PP
With
.B \-W
or
.BR \-t ,
the exit status of
.B su
is that of the shell, any value from 0 to 255, converted like that of
any program that busybox waits for.
A shell that ended as if by a signal has status 128 plus the signal
number, such as 139 for an access violation and 130 for Ctrl+C.
Otherwise the status is the low byte of the Windows exit code, or 255
for a code that is a nonzero multiple of 256.
Such a code above 255 is also reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
su: \fImessage\fR Error 0x\fIcode\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
with the code in hexadecimal, after the Windows description of it when
there is one.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open an elevated shell:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ su
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a command in an elevated window, and keep the window open to read
its output:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ su \-N \-c \(aqfsutil dirty query C:\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Copy a file into a protected directory, and wait for the result.
The operand after
.B root
is the file name, which is
.B $0
in the command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ su \-W \-c \(aqcp "$0" "C:/Program Files/tool/"\(aq root tool.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Try a command line without elevating, to see how the shell receives its
arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ su \-t \-c \(aqecho "$0: $1"\(aq root first second
first: second
.EE
.RE
.PP
Open an elevated
.BR cmd.exe ,
whose path Windows puts in
.BR COMSPEC :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ su \-s "$COMSPEC"
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the Unix
.BR su :
.IP \(bu 2
There is one user,
.BR root ,
which is how busybox names an elevated process.
.BR id (1)
prints
.B uid=0(root)
in the elevated shell.
.IP \(bu 2
Nothing is switched, and
.B su
asks for no password.
Windows decides what the UAC prompt asks and which account the shell
runs as.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no login shell, and none of the Unix options such as
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-m ,
and
.BR \-p .
.B "su \-"
fails with an unknown user.
.IP \(bu 2
The elevated shell does not share the console of
.BR su ,
so nothing can be piped to it or from it.
Use
.B \-W
for its status, and files for its data.
.PP
The default shell is the running busybox executable, started with the
arguments
.BR "\-\-busybox ash" ,
.B \-t
.I title
unless in test mode,
.B \-d
.IR directory ,
then
.B \-N
and
.B \-c
.I command
as given, and the operands; see
.BR sh (1).
The directory is the current directory of
.BR su ,
resolved to its real path first, because a drive mapped to a network
share may not be available once the privilege is raised.
With
.BR \-s ,
no directory is passed, and Windows may start a program that lives in a
system directory, such as
.BR cmd.exe ,
in that directory.
.PP
.B su
quotes each argument by the rules of the Microsoft C runtime, which the
default shell follows.
A shell that parses its command line differently may see them
differently.
.PP
The privilege of a process can be dropped by the busybox applets
.BR drop ,
.BR cdrop ,
or
.BR pdrop ;
see
.BR drop (1).
Windows still reports such a process as elevated, so it would start the
shell without raising the privilege, and
.B su
gives up with status 2.
.B \-t
skips this test.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR drop (1),
.BR id (1),
.BR sh (1)
